The didactic MDP in \cref{fig:didactic-mdp} isolates this problem at state $2$: $a_+$ enters the rewarded cluster A, whereas $a_-$ enters the unrewarded cluster B, before both
branches reach a shared tail. Their future occupancies therefore overlap, but the branch taken determines their difference in return.
We use this setting in \cref{sec:exp-didactic} to evaluate how well FB and \name{} preserve the resulting action-value gap.

Preserving this gap requires the fitted occupancies to retain the difference between visiting the rewarded and unrewarded branches.
Yet \cref{lem:bottleneck-loss} shows that, with backward features fixed, even optimal linear fitting loses this difference when the two occupancies have identical expected
features. This motivates reconsidering how compact features represent future occupancies and how those predictions are fitted.
